# VSCode Terminology & Core Concepts

## Key Types and Concepts

### Editor vs Model

- **Editor** (`ICodeEditor`) - The visual component where you type code
  - Multiple editors can exist (split views)
  - 10 open tabs = still just one editor (unless split)

- **Model** (`ITextModel`) - Internal representation of file contents
  - Shared between editors (changes sync automatically)
  - One model per file, even if displayed in multiple editors

- **URI** (resource) - File path representation
  - Example: `/Users/.../my_file.txt`
  - Use URIs to reference files throughout VSCode

### Application Structure

- **Workbench** - Container for editors, terminal, file tree, panels, etc.
- **Panel** - Bottom area (terminal, problems, output)
- **Sidebar** - Left/right areas (file explorer, extensions, etc.)
- **Auxbar** - Additional auxiliary views

## Actions & Commands

**Actions** (aka **Commands**) are registered functions callable by users or code:

```typescript
registerAction2(class MyAction extends Action2 {
  constructor() {
    super({
      id: 'my.action.id',
      title: 'My Action',
      keybinding: { primary: KeyMod.CtrlCmd | KeyCode.KeyK }
    });
  }
  async run(accessor: ServicesAccessor) {
    // implementation
  }
});
```

- Users invoke via Command Palette (Cmd+Shift+P)
- Code invokes via `commandService.executeCommand(actionId)`
- Keybindings are user-customizable

## Events & Disposables

- Use `Emitter<T>` and `Event<T>` for pub/sub patterns
- Always dispose of subscriptions and event listeners
- Follow the disposable pattern to prevent memory leaks
